<TEI xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
   <teiHeader>
      <fileDesc>
         <titleStmt>
            <title>STELAZINE</title>
         </titleStmt>
         <publicationStmt>
            <p>Publication Information - PAGE 15</p>
         </publicationStmt>
         <sourceDesc>
            <p>Information about the source</p>
         </sourceDesc>
      </fileDesc>
   </teiHeader>
   <text>
      <body>
         <div>
            <p>Sanctioned by franko</p>
            <p>
               <l>In 1963 I was thirteen. My mom was</l>
               <l>a country-western singer and I was a</l>
               <l>farmboy. Also, <name type="artist">the Beatles</name> happened…</l>
               <l>KJR was Radio Free Europe for the</l>
               <l>refugees out in <name type="location">Duvall</name>. To be honest, it</l>
               <l>took another year or so for this</l>
               <l>particular farmkid to catch on to the</l>
               <l>fact that there was a historic change</l>
               <l>happening.</l>
            </p>
            <p>
               <l>When I was in third grade in <name type="location">Sedro</name></l>
               <l><name type="location">Wolley</name>, living next to the movie</l>
               <l>House where “Love Me Tender” and “King</l>
               <l>Creole” played, <name type="artist">Elvis</name> was my main</l>
               <l>muse. Dancing in front of mom's stereo</l>
               <l>to "Hound Dog" and "Don't Be Cruel”..</l>
               <l>groovin’ to the Big Bopper, <name type="artist">Bobby</name></l>
               <l><name type="artist">Darin</name>, <name type="artist">Fats Domino</name> and <name type="artist">Buddy Holly</name> (the</l>
               <l>skinny kid with the glasses) on the</l>
               <l>radio, I had sanction.</l>
            </p>
            <p>
               <l>Mom took me to hear her sing one</l>
               <l>Night because I learned the Presley</l>
               <l>Two-Step (one forward and one back).</l>
            </p>
            <p><l>But <name type="artist">the Beatles</name>, she told me, were</l>
               <l>awful. Dad watched <name type="artist">the Stones</name> on Ed</l>
               <l>Sullivan with us, and said, “I can</l>
               <l>see why they’re called dirty. It’s</l>
               <l>not dirt, it’s Filth!” No sanction.</l>
               <l>But all the time on my Packard-Bell</l>
               <l>portable, up in my little room, I was</l>
               <l>groovin’! To the Folkies. I was</l>
               <l>enraptured and romanced by <name type="artist">Simon’s</name> <name type="song">“I Am</name></l>
               <l><name type="song">A Rock”</name>, and <name type="song">“Young Girls Are Coming</name></l>
               <l><name type="song">To The Canyon”</name> with <name type="artist">the Mamas &amp; Papas</name></l>
               <l><name type="artist">Dylan</name>, yea. And somehow, when <name type="artist">Dylan</name></l>
               <l>picked up an electric guitar I wasn’t</l>
               <l>aware of any outrage. I was down in</l>
               <l>the barnyard, making those sounds</l>
               <l>with my mouth.</l></p>
            <p><l>And I knew those <name type="artist">Beatles</name> couldn’t</l>
               <l>be as awful as mom said. <name type="song">“I Wanna</name></l>
               <l><name type="artist">Hold Your Hand”</name> was the Truth to this</l>
               <l>throbbing pubescent mind.</l></p>
            <p><l>Unbeknownst to me, the youth, and</l>
               <l>quite a few of the grown-ups in</l>
               <l><name type="location">Seattle</name>, <name type="location">Bellevue</name>, <name type="location">Kirkland</name> were on fire</l>
               <l>—Rock N Roll was the wave of the</l>
               <l>future again. And I met them on the</l>
               <l>traditional meeting ground for city and</l>
               <l>country: The county fair.</l></p>
            <p>
               <l>I was in 4-H, of course, and I had</l>
               <l>a pet Jersey cow to lead around.</l>
               <l>Every year we’d go to at least the</l>
               <l><name type="location">Monroe</name> county fair and the <name type="location">King County</name></l>
               <l>Fair in <name type="location">Enumclaw</name>. Although <name type="location">Enumclaw</name></l>
               <l>was the biggest fair, I believe all</l>
               <l>The hip action was in <name type="location">Monroe</name>.</l>
            </p>
            <p>
               <l>There was, of course, the rodeo….</l>
               <l>horse shows, cow shows, and pie shows</l>
               <l>Vacuum cleaners, window cleaners,</l>
               <l>floor cleaners, and organ grinders. But</l>
               <l>there was also stock car races. And</l>
               <l>when there weren’t cars on the track</l>
               <l>the fail officials had found it</l>
               <l>lucrative to bring half-a-dozen <name type="location">Seattle</name></l>
               <l>rock bands to play for free—----there</l>
               <l>at the racetrack.</l>
            </p>
            <p><l>Little did they know the ultimate</l>
               <l>terror they would help bring upon</l>
               <l><name type="location">Washington</name> parents everywhere, as their</l>
               <l>kids became Rock N Rollers, and</l>
               <l>forgot about doing the dishes and/or</l>
               <l>feeding the calves. (I noticed they had</l>
               <l>this much rock n roll only that one</l>
               <l>year. After that they got all the old</l>
               <l>country stars they could find).</l></p>
            <p>
               <l>I must have shoved cow watch onto</l>
               <l>every kid in the club, so me (I was</l>
               <l>the president) and Rick DeVries (he’s</l>
               <l>the vice-president), could go and</l>
               <l>goggle at <name type="artist">Lucky and the Gamblers</name>, the</l>
               <l><name type="artist">Liverpool Five</name>, and numerous other</l>
               <l>tatty costumed bands. Everybody</l>
               <l>looked like <name type="artist">Paul Revere and the Raiders…</name></l>
               <l>everybody in pseudo-Carnaby Street</l>
               <l>duds. And <name type="artist">Merilee Rush> and the</name></l>
               <l><name type="artist">Turnabouts</name>––two shows daily on the midway.</l>
               <l>I was in love with her overbite.</l>
            </p>
            <p><l>And there was this scheme which now</l>
               <l>seems out of a dream. In a tent in</l>
               <l>some accidental corner of the</l>
               <l>fairgrounds, kids from Greater <name type="location">Seattle</name></l>
               <l>burbs—I swear—were having honest-to-god</l>
               <l><hi rend="ul">sanctioned</hi>, amateur Battle of the</l>
               <l>bands. Thirteen year old, sixteen year old</l>
               <l><name type="artist">Johnny Rays</name> and leather <name type="artist">Elvis’s</name>—-</l>
               <l>tight blue jeans and greasy curls,</l>
               <l>were climbing onto this little plywood</l>
               <l>stage with their Fenders and Gibsons</l>
               <l>and singing <name type="song">“House of the Rising Sun”</name></l>
               <l>while their mothers were crawling</l>
               <l>around in their nylons and <name>Jackie</name></l>
               <l><name>Kennedy</name> coats arranging and checking power</l>
               <l>cords. What!!??</l></p>
            <p><graphic url= ""/></p>
            <p>
               <l>No kidding. It was like a 4-H club</l>
               <l>for Rock N Roll. Really, I was out</l>
               <l>there. I don’t know if they won the</l>
               <l>chance to be backup band for the</l>
               <l>Bands at PLU, or if they got to cut</l>
               <l>a single, or if they just got a new</l>
               <l>set of strings. But the point is,</l>
               <l>they were playing rock n roll, and</l>
               <l>their mothers were not only watching, but</l>
               <l>brushing their greasy hair before</l>
               <l>they went on stage. Sanction!</l>
            </p>
            <p>
               <l>They weren’t as “good”, of course, as</l>
               <l>the “Professionals” out on the race</l>
               <l>track; nor did they get the money or</l>
               <l>attention that <name type="artist">Merilee Rush</name> (who</l>
               <l>would pick up her watch to see how much</l>
               <l>longer the set was—right in the</l>
               <l>middle of <name type="song">“Angel Of The Morning”</name>) got…</l>
               <l>but they felt real-er. They were me,</l>
               <l>living my dream. They seemed to be</l>
               <l>not only supported, but even pushed</l>
               <l>by their mothers to perform and live</l>
               <l>out that dream.</l>
            </p>
            <p><l>Sanction.</l></p>
            <p><l>“WE HEARD</l>
               <l>THE</l>
               <l>NEWS</l>
               <l>TODAY,</l>
               <l>OH BOY”</l></p>
            <p><l>FROM JET CITY</l>
               <l>THE NEWS</l>
               <l>BREAKING THE</l>
               <l>SOUND BARRIERS</l></p>
            <p><graphic url= ""/></p>
         </div>
      </body>
   </text>
</TEI>
